            textures and materials on the .cheng (2026-11-08): the model brings its look along now. obj (mtl: Kd, Ns, Ke, map_Kd, Pr / Pm, vt, vertex colours on v lines), gltf / glb (TEXCOORD_0, COLOR_0, pbrMetallicRoughness base colour factor + texture, roughness, metallic, emissive, images in the glb, as data uris or files) and anything through blender (the glb export now carries materials, images embedded). one base-colour texture per .cheng: the one covering the most triangles. a model with several materials folds the rest into vertex colours, and a colour's alpha is its texture weight (1 sample the texture here, 0 the colour alone), so a multi-material blend file still reads right with one texture. the weld keeps a vertex per uv and per colour too, so texture seams and material edges stay two-sided like creases. textures halve until they fit 1024 and go in as a png. format v2: a flags byte after the name (uv / colours / texture), the material (base, roughness, metallic, emissive), uvs as 16-bit inside their own bounds, colours rgba8, the png with its size; v1 files still read. a fourth skin, "own": own.vert / own.frag, uv and colour as attributes 2 and 3 on GLMesh when the mesh has them, rough / metallic blinn-phong with fresnel under the seed's three lights and fog, its emissive on top. and the duotone had to let it through: the whole frame used to go onto the two-colour ramp, so a texture came out as the seed's pink. post has a true colour mode now (the scene target is rgba16f instead of r11g11b10, own.frag writes alpha 0, bloom_duotone keeps the real colour where alpha is 0): the model keeps its colours, the backdrop and bloom stay the seed's. costs a wider scene target, only when a model wears its own look. the app flips a freshly converted model to "own" when it brought something; one that brought nothing shows mono under "own" and the tab says so. plugin, wallpaper mode and the hand-off carry it as any other skin. gltf's metallicFactor default (1.0) is read as 0 when absent: blender's untextured exports came out as mirrors otherwise
            the sandbox: gl version checked (needs 4.3, refuses gles with a hint), fbo clamped to 8192, every qt callback wrapped (render, status, vault, power), render exceptions back off 3 s and give up after 3 in a row until the settings change, the destructor no longer waits on the probe (that stalled plasmashell for the curl timeout on every wallpaper swap)

        gnome still does not offer the layer shell, so on gnome wayland it falls back to the xwayland desktop window, which gnome stacks wrong. that stays the open question in 17. wayland-client, wayland-egl, egl are new deps (arch: wayland, libglvnd. debian: libwayland-dev, libegl-dev)

    gotcha found the hard way: glfw on wayland pulls libdecor, and libdecor's gtk plugin can block forever in glfwInit (it did, in my build container, the day after a reboot). `--no-libdecor` (GLFW_WAYLAND_DISABLE_LIBDECOR init hint) or LIBDECOR_PLUGIN_DIR=/nonexistent. `--x11` is the other escape hatch. the wallpaper mode never hits it because it forces x11

    fps cap in wallpaper mode (30 default), pause when a fullscreen app has focus, pause on battery. a wallpaper that eats the gpu is a wallpaper that gets uninstalled

    multi monitor: one dimension per monitor or the same one mirrored, user picks

    how it landed (v0.7): `chaos --wallpaper`. glfw is forced onto x11 (GLFW_PLATFORM_X11), so a wayland session goes through xwayland without asking. one undecorated, unfocusable window per glfw monitor at the monitor's position and size, created hidden, then via glfw3native + xlib: _NET_WM_WINDOW_TYPE_DESKTOP, _NET_WM_STATE below + sticky + skip taskbar + skip pager, _NET_WM_DESKTOP all, WM_HINTS input False, empty XShape input region. then shown. verified with xprop on kwin's xwayland: desktop type, below, no focus, 1920x1080+0+0, bottom of _NET_CLIENT_LIST_STACKING

    each monitor has its own gl context and its own Renderer. "each" derives the extra seeds as blake3(seed || "monitor" || i), so one seed still describes the whole setup. vsync is off per window and one sleep paces the whole loop at wallpaper_fps (30 default), because vsync on N windows stacks

    pause: every 0.5s it checks _NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW for _NET_WM_STATE_FULLSCREEN and /sys/class/power_supply/*/status for Discharging. paused = sleep 250ms, no frames. (the fullscreen check is written against the ewmh spec, not yet watched with a real game on top. `--no-pause` for testing)

    settings.json (~/.config/chaos/): seed, mode, monitors, wallpaper_fps, pause_on_battery, pause_on_fullscreen, vsync, fps_cap, poster. the panel's settings tab edits it ("use this seed as the wallpaper", save). flags override the file

    autostart: the panel toggles ~/.config/autostart/chaos.desktop with Exec=<this binary> --wallpaper. xdg autostart, every desktop reads it. no systemd unit, maybe later

    no panel and no keys in wallpaper mode on purpose: it has no input region. you drive it from the normal `chaos` window (settings tab) and restart it. a control socket is a v1.3 nicety

    gnome/kde under wayland: still the open question in 17. kwin honours the x11 desktop type through xwayland (tested), gnome does not stack xwayland desktop windows under the shell. that one needs the layer shell or a gnome extension, and I said no extension

